## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract & Introduction

- The abstract and intro overemphasize the novelty of “foundation model training for MILP” when the actual work is three separate task-specific GNNs. This framing is misleading and should be significantly toned down.  
- The motivation that existing ML for MILP is “problem-specific” is largely accurate, but the paper downplays concurrent or recent works that explore multi-class training or more general policies (e.g., some branching works already evaluate on multiple graph-based classes). The difference between “five selected classes” in concurrent work and “> 1000 synthetic classes” here is quantitative rather than qualitative, yet the text describes it as a conceptual leap.

### Section 2 – Learning Tasks

- IG prediction and branching tasks are standard; definitions are fine.  
- For integrality gap, using root-node LP relaxation and normalized difference is reasonable, but the paper does not specify how it handles cases where \(z_{LP}^0 \) is near zero or negative (only hinted in appendix). This matters for interpretability of “deviation” and “correlation”.  
- For learning to branch, the loss is imitation of strong branching, but the main reported metric is solver-level performance (time, % solved). That’s good, but there is little detail on the B&B configuration (cut generation, node selection, presolve) and how sensitive the results are to these choices.

### Section 2.2.3 – Language–MILP Contrastive Learning

- The formal contrastive loss description is standard, but key details are deferred: batch size, negative sampling strategy, and whether temperature is learned vs fixed. These affect whether the model truly learns non-trivial cross-modal structure.  
- There is no description of how text descriptions are created for MIPLIB instances (Section 5.3 just says “meaningful description”). If these descriptions are themselves LLM-generated, that further compounds synthetic-to-synthetic alignment, and weakens claims about real-world interpretability.

### Section 3 – MILP-Evolve

- Core pipeline is described at a high level but many crucial knobs (number of evolution steps per seed, distribution of operator types, stopping criteria) are only in the appendix. For a paper whose main contribution is the data pipeline, the main text is too light on specifics.  
- The filtering procedure is underspecified in the main text: we are told that solve times and feasibility are adjusted, but not by what thresholds or heuristics. There is no analysis of the failure rate of LLM-generated classes (e.g., how many initial generations are infeasible or trivial and thus discarded). This matters because the usable dataset might be a heavily filtered, biased subset of what the LLM proposes.  
- The notion that early-evolution classes are “closer” to seed classes and later ones are “further” is intuitive but unquantified; no metric of class distance is defined beyond the t-SNE of code embeddings.

### Section 4 – Training Pipeline

- The description of the GCN+attention architecture is reasonable and references prior work, but lacks crucial reproducibility details (number of layers, hidden dimensions, sampling rate for nodes in attention, etc.) in the main text. Given that architectural choices appear to materially affect MIPLIB transfer (attention vs no attention), this under-specification is non-trivial.  
- For branching, skipping attention due to runtime overhead is understandable, but then comparing learning-to-branch results to prior SOTA that may not have identical computational budgets is tricky; the paper does not show per-node inference time comparisons or enforce a strict wall-clock compute constraint across methods.

### Section 5 – Experiments

**Baselines and fairness (Sec. 5.1)**

- Seed + Param and Seed + VAE are sensible data baselines, but it is unclear if they are trained with the same *total number of instances* as Ours or are size-limited by what those methods can generate. If Ours simply has both more classes and more total instances, gains may be as much about scale as about diversity.  
- No variance or confidence intervals are reported in Tables 1–3; given the stochastic nature of training and instance generation, this omission makes it hard to assess robustness.

**Results on MILP-Evolve (Sec. 5.2, Table 1, Fig. 4)**

- The improvements are numerically large on the held-out synthetic test set, which is positive. However, these are all within the same synthetic universe. The narrative that “diversity of training data mattering more than number of instances” is only demonstrated within this constrained setting; claims should be clearly qualified accordingly.  
- Fig. 4a distinguishes changing #classes vs #instances per class, but the purple vs grey curves are not precisely described in the main text in terms of exact counts. Without explicit numbers, it is hard to see if changes are proportionally comparable.  

**New unseen MILP-Evolve test set (Table 2)**

- This is a useful robustness check, but the construction—running the same LLM-based pipeline on different seeds—undermines the claim of “unseen” in any deep sense. The text should be clear that this is *within-pipeline* transfer, not transfer to independently created benchmarks.  

**MIPLIB transfer (Sec. 5.3, Table 3, Fig. 5)**

- The improvements on MIPLIB are credible and show that pretraining on a large synthetic corpus can help. However:
  - There is no ablation on which aspects of MILP-Evolve matter (number of classes, application-type domain, use of advanced modeling techniques, etc.).  
  - The models seemingly fine-tune to convergence; faster convergence with Ours is nice, but that alone does not make a foundation model—any reasonable pretraining usually helps optimization.  
  - Absence of learning-to-branch on MIPLIB is understandable given the data scarcity, but it also means that the most solver-relevant task is only evaluated on synthetic data. This weakens the claim of practical impact on real-world solving.

### Related Work

- Positioning vis-à-vis recent data-generation methods (VAE, diffusion for MILPs) is fair, but the paper could be more explicit that those works focus on instance-level variation while this focuses on class-level variation.  
- The discussion of LLMs for MILP does not fully acknowledge that some prior works do access and reason about A, b, c in various ways; the blanket statement that prior work is “data-agnostic” is somewhat oversimplified.

### Conclusion

- The conclusion explicitly acknowledges that models are task-specific and that unifying tasks into a single model is future work. This acknowledgement is at odds with the stronger “foundation model” rhetoric in the title and abstract. The paper should be internally consistent and present itself as a data-generation and multi-class pretraining study, not a foundation-model breakthrough.

---

## Strengths

- **Clear and relevant problem framing:** The paper addresses a real and important issue in ML for MILP: generalization beyond narrowly defined problem classes.  
- **Novel use of LLMs for MILP *class* generation:** The MILP-Evolve concept—using LLMs with evolutionary prompts to generate *new problem classes* rather than just instances—is genuinely interesting and, if better validated, could be influential.  
- **Three complementary tasks:** Evaluating on integrality gap prediction, learning to branch, and MILP–text alignment gives a reasonably broad view of what the learned representations can do within the chosen scope.  
- **Empirical evidence of pretraining benefits:** Across synthetic data and MIPLIB, pretraining on a large synthetic corpus does improve performance and convergence relative to training from scratch and to smaller baselines.  
- **Attention-based architecture for MILP graphs:** The combination of GCN and attention (with summary nodes) appears to yield measurable benefits on transfer; this is a sensible, well-motivated architectural choice.

---

## Overall Assessment

The paper contains a genuinely promising idea—LLM-based *class*-level MILP generation—and provides initial evidence that training on many synthetic classes can improve multi-class performance and transfer to MIPLIB. However, the central “towards foundation models for MILP” framing is not supported: the models are task-specific, the evidence for true cross-domain generalization is limited, and the diversity and realism of the MILP-Evolve dataset are not rigorously characterized. Baseline comparisons confound data diversity with other factors, and claims about interpretability improvements via language alignment are overstated given the narrow multiple-choice evaluation. In its current form, this is a solid and interesting data/benchmark paper with overambitious framing, not a convincing step toward foundation models. I would not recommend acceptance unless the claims are significantly downscoped and the evaluation more rigorously disentangles data diversity from architecture/scale, and includes stronger evidence about the structure and realism of the generated MILP classes.
### Neutral Reviewer
## Balanced Review

### Summary

This paper proposes a foundation model approach for Mixed Integer Linear Programming (MILP) learning, introducing **MILP-Evolve**, an LLM-based evolutionary framework that generates diverse MILP problem classes from seed classes via operators such as adding/deleting constraints, crossover, mutation, and new class creation. A single GNN-based model (with optional attention) is trained on the diverse generated data and evaluated across three tasks—integrality gap prediction, learning to branch, and a novel language-MILP contrastive learning task—showing substantial improvements over baselines on held-out MILP-Evolve classes and transfer learning to MIPLIB benchmarks.

### Strengths

1. **Novel and important problem framing.** The paper identifies a genuine, well-documented limitation in current ML-for-MILP work: class-specific models that fail to generalize across problem classes (e.g., a Set Cover model failing on Facility Location). The foundation model framing is a meaningful conceptual shift for the field, supported by clear motivation in Section 1.

2. **MILP-Evolve is creative and well-designed.** Using LLMs to evolve MILP classes from seeds with 10 diverse prompt operators is innovative. The modular class representation (Data, Optimization Modeling, Parameters) enabling systematic evolution and filtering is well thought out. The t-SNE visualization (Figure 1b) convincingly demonstrates that generated classes cover a broader embedding space than seeds.

3. **Strong empirical results on held-out classes.** Table 1 shows substantial gains: 5.8× correlation improvement for IG prediction (0.10→0.58), ~1.42× improvement in instances solved for branching (49.59%→70.90%), and ~1.92× improvement for 4-way language alignment accuracy (37.21%→70.54%). These are meaningful, not marginal, improvements.

4. **Important insight: class diversity matters more than instance quantity.** Figure 4 provides clear evidence: reducing classes while keeping total instances constant significantly hurts performance (Figure 4a, purple vs. gray), while reducing instances per class has minimal effect. This is a valuable finding for the field.

5. **Novel task introduction.** The language-MILP contrastive learning task (Section 2.2.3) is well-motivated—making MILP accessible to non-experts—and the CLIP-inspired contrastive framework is a natural design choice. The observation that this approach significantly outperforms GPT-4o direct interpretation (Appendix A.3.7) adds practical value.

6. **Transfer learning to MIPLIB demonstrates real-world relevance.** Tables 2 and 3 show that pretraining on MILP-Evolve data improves fine-tuning on both unseen seed classes and MIPLIB, with faster convergence (Figure 5), suggesting the generated data captures useful structural diversity.

7. **Reproducibility.** Code and data are publicly available at the provided GitHub link.

### Weaknesses

1. **"Foundation model" terminology is overstated relative to what is delivered.** The paper trains **separate models** for each task—one for IG, one for branching, one for language alignment. A true foundation model would be a single model handling all tasks. The authors acknowledge this in the conclusion ("we acknowledge that this work still trains separate models for each learning task"), but the title ("Towards Foundation Models") and abstract framing are quite ambitious for what is essentially multi-class, task-specific learning. The gap between the framing and the actual contribution could mislead readers.

2. **MILP-Evolve relies heavily on GPT-4o with limited cost/transparency discussion.** The pipeline depends on a proprietary, frontier LLM, but the paper does not report how many LLM calls were made, the computational cost, or the fraction of generated classes that passed/failed filtering. This limits reproducibility assessments and scalability considerations. The paper states "more than a thousand" classes but does not specify the exact number or the yield rate.

3. **MIPLIB evaluation for branching is omitted.** The branching task is arguably the most practically important MILP learning task, yet the paper excludes it from MIPLIB evaluation because only 13 instances had solve times in the appropriate range (20s–300s). This is a significant gap in the evaluation, as MIPLIB is the standard heterogeneous benchmark. The paper could have adapted the evaluation protocol (e.g., relaxing time bounds, using different metrics like node count reduction) rather than dropping the task entirely.

4. **Limited analysis of the attention mechanism.** The paper states that attention "improves performance, especially for transfer learning to the MIPLIB dataset" and that this is "expected," but provides no deeper analysis—no visualization of attention patterns, no ablation on attention design choices (e.g., subsampling strategy, number of attention heads), and no explanation of why attention specifically aids transfer versus within-distribution performance.

5. **Language-MILP task evaluation depth is limited.** The 4-way and 10-way accuracy metrics for contrastive learning are reasonable but do not fully assess alignment quality. Missing are retrieval metrics (e.g., recall@k), qualitative analysis of matched/unmatched pairs, and evaluation of whether learned embeddings enable downstream applications (e.g., zero-shot classification, text-to-MILP retrieval).

6. **Comparison with Huang et al. (2024) is superficial.** The concurrent work is mentioned as training on five classes, but no direct experimental comparison is provided. Given that both papers address multi-class MILP learning, a head-to-head comparison would significantly strengthen the evaluation.

7. **No comparison with alternative GNN architectures.** The paper uses GCN throughout but does not compare with GAT, GraphSAGE, or other architectures that might better capture variable-constraint interactions in MILP graphs. This leaves the architectural choice unsubstantiated beyond following prior work.

### Novelty & Significance

The paper is **novel** in three distinct ways: (1) the foundation model framing for MILP is new and timely; (2) MILP-Evolve as an LLM-based evolutionary MILP class generator is a creative methodological contribution; (3) the language-MILP contrastive learning task is a novel problem formulation with practical motivation. The insight that class diversity outweighs instance quantity is also significant for the field.

The **significance** is high because generalization across MILP classes is a real bottleneck preventing practical adoption of ML-for-MILP. The improvements on unseen classes and MIPLIB transfer are meaningful. However, significance is somewhat tempered by the task-specific model training (rather than a unified foundation model) and the incomplete MIPLIB branching evaluation. The work represents a genuine step forward, but the "foundation model" claim is aspirational rather than fully realized.

### Suggestions for Improvement

1. **Clarify and moderate the foundation model framing.** Either adjust the title/abstract to more accurately reflect multi-class learning with task-specific models, or add a substantial discussion section analyzing what would be required for a true unified foundation model (architecture, training scheme, task interfaces) and how far the current work is from that goal.

2. **Provide detailed MILP-Evolve statistics.** Report the exact number of classes generated, the number passing each filter stage, common failure modes, LLM call counts, and approximate computational cost. This is essential for reproducibility and for assessing whether the approach is practically scalable.

3. **Strengthen MIPLIB branching evaluation.** Rather than dropping the task, adapt the evaluation: use node count reduction as a metric (which doesn't require specific solve time ranges), evaluate on all solvable MIPLIB instances, or clearly frame this as a key limitation with concrete proposed solutions for future work.

4. **Deepen attention mechanism analysis.** Add ablations on attention design (subsampling ratio, heads, pooling strategy), visualize attention patterns on representative MILP instances, and provide a mechanistic explanation for why attention aids transfer learning specifically.

5. **Expand language-MILP evaluation.** Add recall@k retrieval metrics, qualitative examples of correct and incorrect matches, and at least one downstream task evaluation (e.g., zero-shot classification of MILP instances by domain).

6. **Include direct comparison with Huang et al. (2024).** If feasible, reproduce their multi-class training setup and compare directly on shared benchmarks, or at minimum provide a detailed methodological comparison table.

7. **Compare alternative GNN architectures.** Run at least GAT and GraphSAGE comparisons on key tasks to validate the GCN choice and provide guidance for future architecture selection.

8. **Discuss scalability without frontier LLMs.** Analyze whether smaller/open-source LLMs can produce comparable class diversity, and discuss the long-term viability of the approach as LLM capabilities evolve.
### Spark
## How to Improve This Paper

### Missing Experiments (top 3-5 only)
1. **No comparison against class-specific SOTA models on learning to branch.** The paper only compares multi-class baselines on the same GCN architecture. Without showing how a single foundation model compares to per-class trained models (e.g., Gasse et al. 2019 trained individually), we cannot assess whether the foundation model approach sacrifices per-class performance — a core tradeoff the paper must address.

2. **Learning to branch is entirely missing on MIPLIB.** This is one of three core tasks, and the only one directly tied to solver acceleration (the most practical claim). Omitting it on the most recognized real-world benchmark, with only a brief justification, significantly weakens the "foundation model for MILP" narrative.

3. **No comparison with the concurrent multi-class work (Huang et al., 2024).** The paper explicitly mentions this concurrent work as the only prior attempt at joint multi-class training but provides no experimental comparison. This is a direct competitor that must be benchmarked against.

4. **No reporting of MILP-Evolve generation yield or failure rates.** We are told 1000+ classes are generated but not how many were attempted, how many failed syntax/feasibility filtering, or what fraction of LLM outputs were discarded. Without this, the scalability and practicality of MILP-Evolve cannot be assessed.

### Deeper Analysis Needed (top 3-5 only)
1. **Quantitative diversity metrics for MILP-Evolve classes.** Diversity is the central claim, yet it is supported only by a t-SNE plot. Report structural statistics across generated classes: constraint matrix sparsity patterns, variable-to-constraint ratios, integrality gap distributions, constraint types, etc. Without this, "1000+ classes" could be near-duplicates with trivial perturbations.

2. **Analysis of performance vs. evolutionary depth/distance from seeds.** MILP-Evolve generates classes at varying depths from seeds. Does model performance degrade on deeper-evolved classes? This would reveal whether the diversity is actually beneficial or just adds noise.

3. **Per-class or per-category breakdown of test performance.** Aggregate metrics on the held-out set hide where the model succeeds vs. fails. Report performance stratified by problem category or structural properties to understand generalization boundaries.

4. **Integrality gap prediction quality is modest (correlation 0.58) with no error analysis.** This correlation indicates the predictions explain only ~34% of variance. Analysis of when and why predictions fail (e.g., large-gap vs. small-gap instances, specific problem structures) is needed to assess practical utility.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Concrete examples of evolved MILP classes at different depths.** The paper shows one evolution chain (Figure 3) but no actual generated problem formulations. Showing 5-10 representative evolved classes with their constraint structures would reveal whether MILP-Evolve produces meaningful vs. superficial diversity.

2. **Learned embedding visualizations by class and task.** Show t-SNE/UMAP of the model's representations (not just raw code embeddings), colored by class and task performance, to demonstrate whether the foundation model learns meaningful shared structure vs. memorizes per-class features.

3. **Failure case analysis for learning to branch.** The branching task shows negative time improvement for baselines. Show specific instances where the foundation model helps vs. hurts solver performance, with B&B tree visualizations, to understand when multi-class learning transfers effectively.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Evaluate learning to branch on additional real-world heterogeneous benchmarks** beyond MIPLIB (e.g., Coral, MiniZinc challenge instances) to provide any evidence that the branching policy transfers to practical settings.

2. **Train a single unified model across all three tasks** rather than three separate models. The paper explicitly acknowledges this gap in the conclusion but it directly undermines the "foundation model" framing — a foundation model should handle multiple tasks, not require separate training per task.

3. **Compare against simply scaling up the number of instances in seed classes with a larger model.** The diversity-vs-quantity claim needs a stronger test: hold total compute constant and compare training a larger model on more seed instances vs. a standard model on diverse classes.

### Other stuff you can add
1. **Report computational cost of MILP-Evolve generation.** LLM API calls, filtering compute, and total generation time would help practitioners assess feasibility.
2. **Investigate contamination between MILP-Evolve and MIPLIB.** Since LLMs trained on internet data generate the MILP classes, there is a risk that some generated classes inadvertently resemble MIPLIB instances, inflating transfer learning results.
### Human Finder
Based on my analysis of the submitted paper and relevant human-written reviews from similar papers, here are the strengths and weaknesses:

---

## Strengths

1. **Novel and ambitious problem framing**: The paper takes a first step toward foundation models for MILP, proposing to train a single model across diverse MILP problem classes. This addresses a real limitation in prior ML-for-MILP work, which trains class-specific models. Reviewers of similar generalist CO models (GOAL, RouteFinder) consistently noted the importance and novelty of this direction.

2. **MILP-Evolve as a data generation contribution**: The LLM-based evolutionary pipeline is a creative and practical solution to the MILP data scarcity problem. Generating diverse problem classes (rather than just instances within a class) is a genuine innovation over prior instance-augmentation methods (Seed+VAE, Seed+Param).

3. **Comprehensive multi-task evaluation**: Evaluating across three distinct tasks (integrality gap prediction, learning to branch, language-MILP alignment) provides stronger evidence than single-task evaluations. The scaling analysis showing class diversity matters more than instance quantity (Figure 4) is a valuable insight.

4. **Positive transfer learning results**: The MIPLIB transfer learning results (Table 3) and convergence plots (Figure 5) demonstrate that pretraining on diverse MILP-Evolve data provides a meaningful initialization advantage.

---

## Weaknesses

1. **Overclaim of "foundation model" while training separate task-specific models**: The title and framing heavily emphasize "foundation models," yet the paper trains three separate models for three separate tasks. A true foundation model would be a single model handling multiple tasks. As noted in reviews of similar generalist CO papers, the paper "still trains separate models for each learning task," making the foundation model claim aspirational rather than delivered. Reviewers of the GOAL paper similarly noted that "There is still a significant gap between GOAL and the optimal solutions" and questioned whether "the time of 'general solvers' has not yet arrived." *(Inspired by: z2z9suDRjw, Reviewer 1, Weaknesses: "There is still a significant gap between GOAL and the optimal solutions...the gap between GOAL and the best solver may make GOAL useless in solving real-world problems. Such a gap may also suggest that the time of 'general solvers' has not yet arrived.")*

2. **Insufficient evaluation on real-world benchmarks, especially for learning to branch**: The paper omits the branching task on MIPLIB entirely because only 13 instances met the time criteria, acknowledging this is "too small" an evaluation set. This means the most practically important task has no real-world benchmark evaluation. Reviewers of similar MILP learning papers have noted that evaluation on only synthetic/homogeneous problems fails to demonstrate real-world utility. *(Inspired by: du9reSRIo1, Reviewer 3, Weaknesses: "The foundation model is not validated comprehensively...ignoring more practical vehicle routing problems like Solomon VRPTW data" and Reviewer 4: "there is not yet a neural method that could be trained on a single VRP variant...and outperform the strong traditional method...then what is the point in training a unified model across multiple tasks with much performance drop?")*

3. **Lack of comparison against strong task-specific baselines**: The baselines (Seed, Seed+Param, Seed+VAE) are all trained on limited class sets. The paper does not compare against strong specialized models for each task (e.g., strong branching-specific methods like CAMBranch or other state-of-the-art branching approaches). Without showing competitive performance vs. specialized models, it is unclear whether the foundation model approach offers practical value. *(Inspired by: Kc3yoIL5oR, Reviewer 3, Weakness W4: "the more important thing seems that the proposed model is very efficient, significantly reducing inference time" but "performance gain...does not seem significant" vs specialized solvers; and YhwDw31DGI, Reviewer 1: "The authors did not compare with simple data augmentation techniques, such as randomly perturb the MIP instance parameters.")*

4. **Quality and validity concerns of LLM-generated MILP classes**: The paper relies on GPT-4o to generate diverse and valid MILP problem classes, but provides limited analysis of whether the generated classes are mathematically sound, non-trivial, and non-redundant. The filtering step (syntax filter, parameter search) is described at a high level but lacks rigorous quality analysis. Prior reviews of LLM-generated synthetic data raised concerns about "self-verification" and whether LLM-generated content truly captures meaningful diversity or simply superficial variations. *(Inspired by: oqsQbn4XfT, Reviewer 2, Weaknesses: "Using the same model to verify its own reasoning chain and clustering judgements may lead to a positive bias; the verification module would benefit from utilizing an LLM that is not part of the earlier steps"; and miGpIhquyB, Reviewer 3: "It is possible that the models used for generating datasets have seen the reference datasets either during pretraining or instruction-tuning. This would inflate the quality measures.")*

5. **Limited architectural novelty and lack of ablation analysis on the attention mechanism**: The GNN+attention architecture is standard, and the attention module's contribution appears marginal in some tasks (e.g., Table 1a: deviation 20.82% without attention vs. 20.14% with attention; Table 1b: 4-way acc 70.41% vs. 70.54%). The paper does not sufficiently analyze when and why attention helps or its computational cost. *(Inspired by: du9reSRIo1, Reviewer 2, Weaknesses: "The improvements to the Transformer architecture are mainly module-level substitutions...While these changes enhance training stability and convergence, they largely involve the application of existing techniques rather than introducing significant innovation" and z2z9suDRjw, Reviewer 1: "The contribution to the foundational neural model for CO appears to be somewhat limited, as it seems to utilize the same neural network architecture as MatNet.")*

6. **The Language-MILP contrastive learning task evaluation is narrow**: The 4-way and 10-way accuracy metrics for language alignment are relatively simple retrieval tasks. The practical utility of this task is unclear beyond a proof-of-concept—the paper states the task can "serve as an important stepping-stone" toward generating natural language from MILPs, but does not demonstrate this next step. The descriptions being LLM-generated means the task may be easier than aligning with human-written descriptions. *(Inspired by: miGpIhquyB, Reviewer 2, Weaknesses: "This study only focuses on synthetic data generation for relatively simple classification tasks. It would be great if this work can include evaluation on some more complex tasks.")*

7. **Computational cost of data generation pipeline is undisclosed**: The MILP-Evolve pipeline uses GPT-4o to generate over a thousand MILP classes, each requiring multiple LLM calls. The paper does not report the cost, time, or number of API calls needed. This is important for reproducibility and practical adoption. Similarly, the computational cost of training and inference (particularly the overhead of the attention mechanism during branch-and-bound) is not thoroughly analyzed. *(Inspired by: oqsQbn4XfT, Reviewer 1: "What would be the average price for carrying out such an experiment due to the presence of multiple proprietary LLM calls?" and gyvYKLEm8t, Reviewer 1: "Consider providing an analysis of the computational overhead introduced by the tripartite graph representation.")*

